  "query_preview": "..\n(This evening through next Tuesday)\nIssued at 1217 PM CDT Wed Oct 7 2026\n\nA beautiful day is progressing across the Mid-South with\ntemperatures currently spanning the upper 70s to low 80s.\nGenerally dry and pleasant, albeit a little warm, weather\nconditions are expected through this upcoming Friday as weak\nupper-level, northwesterly flow shifts to a more zonal flow\npattern. High temperatures for today are still on track to reach\nthe 80s for much of the area, with a few locations closing in on\nthe low 90s across our southern tier of counties. High\ntemperatures will likely be a few more degrees higher on Thursday\nand Friday, with much of the Mid-South in the upper 80s to low\n90s. While these values are several degrees above normal for\nearly to mid October, dew point temperatures will remain in the\n50s, so it will not feel too unpleasant outside.\n\nLate Friday night and into Saturday, our focus will turn to the\nsouth as Isaias moves onshore. There is still significant\nuncertainty with the eventual path and strength of the system,\nespecially as it gets enveloped with the upper-level flow\npattern. As things stand for the Mid-South, the main concerns\nrelated to the eventual remnants of Isaias will be gusty winds\nand heavy rainfall. Both of these threats appear to be most\nprominent east of the Mississippi River, especially across\nnortheast Mississippi and along the TN River. The westward extent\nof these impacts will be greatly dependent on the eventual track\nof the circulation itself, which is still a day or two away from\nbeing fully resolved. Most of any impacts would be through the\ndaytime on Saturday and into the late evening hours. As things\nstand, wind gusts of 20 to 30 mph seem likely, with the chance of\nwind gusts exceeding 40 mph currently less than 10% for our area.\nAs far as heavy rainfall, this will be highly dependent on the\npath of the remnants as well. Generally 1 to 3 inches of rainfall\nappears to be the most probable, with the lowest amounts west and\nhighest amounts east. Latest NBM guidance does have a 30 to 40%\nchance of rainfall amounts exceeding 3 inches across northeast\nMississippi, which would likely be the high end of the event. If\nhigher end totals were to materialize, this would increase the\nthreat for flooding. Any threat for severe thunderstorms or\ntornadoes will likely remain to the east of our area with the\ncurrent projected path.\n\nBy Sunday afternoon, any lingering remnants of Isaias will\nquickly push off to our northeast, leaving behind a dry and\nseasonable forecast into next week. Zonal flow will develop\naloft, with a surface high pressure developing into the middle of\nnext week. This will keep high temperatures just above normal for\nthe middle of October, with high temperatures spanning the 80s.",
